ojs - okernel's JavaScript engine
=================================

Written from scratch for okernel (no third-party code). It runs page scripts
for the okai browser (src/web/wjs*.c embeds it, one realm per document) and
builds the same on the host for testing (tests/ojs/build.sh).

Layout
------
  ojs.h            embedding API (values, eval/call, objects, roots, modules)
  ojs_int.h        internal types: values, strings, objects, shapes, intrinsics
  gc.c             heap: size-class pages + large blocks, non-moving mark-sweep
                   with a conservative scan of the C stack and of VM frames
  str.c            strings (latin-1 / UTF-16, ropes), atoms, string builder
  obj.c            ordinary objects: shapes (shared transitions, dictionaries),
                   fast elements, property definitions
  lex.c parse.c parse_stmt.c   source -> AST (scopes, early errors)
  compile*.c       AST -> bytecode (per-function templates, line tables)
  vm.c vm_run.c vm_helpers.c   the interpreter (direct-threaded, inline caches)
  realm.c b_*.c generator.c module.c   the built-ins, generators/async, modules
  re.c b_regexp.c  regular expressions (backtracking matcher, v-mode sets)
  num.c bigint.c   number <-> string (exact), BigInt
  srctext.c        compact (UTF-8) source text kept for compiled functions
  unicode.c unicode_data.c     Unicode tables; unicode_data.c is generated
                   from the Unicode Character Database by tools/gen_unicode.py
                   (data, not code)
  intl.c intl.h b_intl.c intl_data.c   ECMA-402 (Intl): intl.c is the runtime
                   (BCP 47 grammar + canonicalization + matching, plural-rule
                   evaluation, decimal rounding on the exact expansion of a
                   double, the CLDR number/date pattern engines), b_intl.c the
                   JS surface, intl_data.c the CLDR tables generated by
                   tools/gen_intl.py from CLDR JSON (third-party DATA under
                   the Unicode License, like unicode_data.c; the sources are
                   re-generated with `python3 tools/gen_intl.py`, which reads
                   build-host/cldr/)

Things to know
--------------
* Values are NaN-boxed 64-bit words (ojsv). No reference counting: the
  collector scans the C stack between ojs_enter() and the current frame, and
  the VM value stack conservatively; C heap memory holding values must be
  registered (ojs_add_root / ojs_add_root_range).
* SSE2 doubles on x86-64 (thread context only in the kernel: interrupt handlers do not save XMM). double -> int conversions
  still go through the IEEE bits (d2i64, dtoi32, jv_number), which is also what the 32-bit x87 Windows port needs.
* Inline caches (struct ic, IC_WAYS entries per property/global site) are
  made on first use and survive collections: ftempl_trace marks what their
  entries name (shape, holder, proto, key), so entries never point at freed
  objects and hot sites stay warm across the 20-30 collections of a big
  page load. Entries holding a shape die with their template; a proto-chain
  entry re-checks the realm's proto epoch, so a prototype change invalidates
  it without a collection. Because a minor collection does not trace old
  templates, every IC fill on an old template records the template
  (ic_barrier), so the next minor's remembered-set drain re-runs
  ftempl_trace over what the entries name.
* The collector has a generational path (gc_collect_minor): per-object
  generations (GCF_OLD/GCF_AGE), write barriers at every mutation funnel
  (gc_wb / gc_wb_p in ojs_int.h; the JIT emits its own after PUT_FIELD and
  PUT_ELEM), a remembered set (gc_remember), promotion with a scan, and a
  young-only sweep that leaves the allocator's avail lists alone. A full
  collection demotes every survivor, so the old-generation invariant ("old
  objects with young children are recorded") holds vacuously after it.
  It is OPT-IN (OJS_GC_GEN=1): a plain churn workload still corrupts with
  minors on (mini2.js in build-host/night reproduces: 400x2000 object
  pushes, unmarked-reachable checks pass, so the bug is a write, not a
  free). Debug knobs: OJS_GC_VERIFY re-traces everything marked ignoring
  generations before the sweep, OJS_GC_VERIFY with the sweep's own check
  aborts on a free of a referenced object, OJS_GC_POISON poisons freed
  slots, OJS_GC_NOAGE/OJS_GC_NOBARRIER bisect aging and the barriers.
  The full collector is unaffected: test262, corpus and smoke are
  byte-identical with minors off.
* The realm memory limit (ojs_set_memory_limit) counts memory taken from the
  system, ArrayBuffer storage included (gc_ext_alloc); an allocation that
  would pass it collects first. ojs_set_memory_guard lets the embedder veto
  growth (the kernel keeps a reserve free for itself).
* A function can have more than 65536 constants (a big bundle's wrapper):
  CONST has a u16 index and CONST_W a u32 one; all other const operands are u32.
* Templates keep their script's source (toString, stack traces). A big script
  that needed UTF-16 is re-stored as UTF-8 after compiling (srctext.c), so it
  costs about its UTF-8 size, not twice that.

Tests
-----
  sh tests/ojs/build.sh                 host build -> build-host/ojs_run
  ./build-host/ojs_run tests/ojs/smoke.js           (prints ALL OK)
  python3 tests/ojs/run262.py -j8 test/language     test262 (~/test262)
  python3 tests/ojs/corpus/run_corpus.py            real libraries, node vs ojs
  OJS_GCSTRESS=N   collect every N allocations (any runner)
  OJS_GCSTATS=1    heap / live bytes by type at exit
  OJS_DUMP=1       bytecode listing
  OJS_MEMLIMIT=MB  realm limit in ojs_run (like a page)
The corpus downloads third-party libraries into build-host/corpus-lib as test
input only; they are never part of the kernel.
